Fix silent wire-parser desync in keyd-application-mapper.

The Wayland reader used bare self.sock.recv(8) / self.sock.recv(size-8).
On a SOCK_STREAM recv() may return fewer bytes than requested; when the
payload read comes up short (a message straddling a socket-buffer
boundary, common under window-title churn over a long session) the next
recv_msg reads the leftover bytes as a fresh header and the stream
desyncs permanently. The process stays alive and connected but never
matches a window again, so per-app bindings silently stop applying.

Route both reads through a recvall() loop. Mirrors upstream PR #1261.

--- a/scripts/keyd-application-mapper
+++ b/scripts/keyd-application-mapper
@@ -215,13 +215,25 @@
         self.sock.sendall(struct.pack(b'II', object_id, opcode))
         self.sock.sendall(payload)
 
+    def _recvall(self, n):
+        # SOCK_STREAM recv() may return fewer bytes than requested; loop
+        # until the whole message is read. A short read otherwise desyncs
+        # the parser and the mapper silently stops reacting to focus.
+        buf = b''
+        while len(buf) < n:
+            chunk = self.sock.recv(n - len(buf))
+            if not chunk:
+                raise EOFError('wayland socket closed')
+            buf += chunk
+        return buf
+
     def recv_msg(self):
-        (object_id, evcode) = struct.unpack('II', self.sock.recv(8))
+        (object_id, evcode) = struct.unpack('II', self._recvall(8))
 
         size = evcode >> 16
         evcode = evcode & 0xFFFF
 
-        data = self.sock.recv(size-8)
+        data = self._recvall(size-8)
 
         return (object_id, evcode, data)
 
